      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2102.03334_p2_b23",
      "text": "Figure 2. Four categories of vision-and-language models. The height of each rectangle denotes its relative computational size. VE, TE,\n",
      "bbox": [
        54.94,
        204.05,
        542.56,
        213.06
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        219,
        816,
        2170,
        852
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 8.97,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu-Slant",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 134,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2102.03334_p2_b24",
      "text": "and MI are short for visual embedder, textual embedder, and modality interaction, respectively.\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.44,
        215.04,
        395.8,
        224.01
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        221,
        860,
        1583,
        896
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 8.97,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 95,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2102.03334_p2_b25",
      "text": "BERT (Huang et al., 2020) is one exception of this trend,\nas it uses ResNet variants (He et al., 2016; Xie et al., 2017)\npre-trained on ImageNet classi\ufb01cation (Russakovsky et al.,\n2015) embedding pixels in place of object detection mod-\nules.\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.44,
        251.02,
        291.09,
        308.88
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        221,
        1004,
        1164,
        1235
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 242,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2102.03334_p2_b26",
      "text": "To this date, most VLP studies have focused on improving\nperformance by increasing the power of visual embedders.\nThe shortcomings of having a heavy visual embedder are\noften disregarded in academic experiments because region\nfeatures are commonly cached in advance at training time\nto ease the burden of feature extraction. However, the lim-\nitations are still evident in real-world applications as the\nqueries in the wild have to undergo a slow extraction pro-\ncess.\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.13,
        316.82,
        291.18,
        422.45
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        220,
        1267,
        1164,
        1689
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.0,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 468,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2102.03334_p2_b27",
      "text": "To this end, we shift our attention to the lightweight and fast\nembedding of visual inputs. Recent work (Dosovitskiy et al.,\n2020; Touvron et al., 2020) demonstrated that using a simple\nlinear projection of a patch is effective enough to embed\npixels before feeding them into transformers. Whereas being\nthe solid mainstream for text (Devlin et al., 2019), it is only\nrecently that transformers (Vaswani et al., 2017) are used for\nimages as well. We presume that the transformer module\u2013\nused for modality interaction in VLP models\u2013 can also\nmanage to process visual features in place of a convolutional\nvisual embedder, just as it processes textual features.\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.13,
        430.5,
        290.94,
        559.94
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        220,
        1721,
        1163,
        2239
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.86,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 658,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2102.03334_p2_b28",
      "text": "This paper proposes the Vision-and-Language Transformer\n(ViLT) that handles two modalities in a single uni\ufb01ed man-\nner. It mainly differs from previous VLP models in its\nshallow, convolution-free embedding of pixel-level inputs.\nRemoving deep embedders solely dedicated to visual in-\nputs signi\ufb01cantly cuts down the model size and running\ntime by design. Figure 1 shows that our parameter-ef\ufb01cient\nmodel is tens of times faster than VLP models with region\nfeatures and at least four times faster than those with grid\nfeatures while exhibiting similar or even better performance\non vision-and-language downstream tasks.\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.11,
        567.93,
        291.18,
        697.42
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        220,
        2271,
        1164,
        2789
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.94,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 618,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2102.03334_p2_b29",
      "text": "Our key contributions can be summarized as follows:\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.44,
        705.39,
        267.52,
        715.35
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        221,
        2821,
        1070,
        2861
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 51,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2102.03334_p2_b30",
      "text": "\u2022 ViLT is the simplest architecture by far for a vision-\nand-language model as it commissions the transformer\nmodule to extract and process visual features in place\nof a separate deep visual embedder. This design in-\nherently leads to signi\ufb01cant runtime and parameter\nef\ufb01ciency.\n",
      "bbox": [
        318.9,
        250.84,
        543.09,
        320.83
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1275,
        1003,
        2172,
        1283
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 278,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2102.03334_p2_b31",
      "text": "\u2022 For the \ufb01rst time, we achieve competent performance\non vision-and-language tasks without using region fea-\ntures or deep convolutional visual embedders in gen-\neral.\n",
      "bbox": [
        318.9,
        332.4,
        543.1,
        378.26
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1275,
        1329,
        2172,
        1513
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 167,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2102.03334_p2_b32",
      "text": "\u2022 Also, for the \ufb01rst time, we empirically show that whole\nword masking and image augmentations that were un-\nprecedented in VLP training schemes further drive\ndownstream performance.\n",
      "bbox": [
        318.9,
        389.86,
        543.1,
        435.68
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1275,
        1559,
        2172,
        1742
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 182,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2102.03334_p2_b33",
      "text": "2. Background\n",
      "bbox": [
        307.44,
        452.67,
        382.29,
        464.62
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1229,
        1810,
        1529,
        1858
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 13,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2102.03334_p2_b34",
      "text": "2.1. Taxonomy of Vision-and-Language Models\n",
      "bbox": [
        307.44,
        473.57,
        507.64,
        483.53
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1229,
        1894,
        2030,
        1934
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 43,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2102.03334_p2_b35",
      "text": "We propose a taxonomy of vision-and-language models\nbased on two points: (1) whether the two modalities have an\neven level of expressiveness in terms of dedicated parame-\nters and/or computation; and (2) whether the two modalities\ninteract in a deep network. A combination of these points\nleads to four archetypes in Figure 2.\n",
      "bbox": [
        306.97,
        492.24,
        543.09,
        562.05
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1227,
        1968,
        2172,
        2248
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 326,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2102.03334_p2_b36",
      "text": "The visual semantic embedding (VSE) models such as\nVSE++ (Faghri et al., 2017) and SCAN (Lee et al., 2018)\nbelong to Figure 2a. They use separate embedders for image\nand text, with the former being much heavier. Then, they\nrepresent the similarity of the embedded features from the\ntwo modalities with simple dot products or shallow attention\nlayers.\n",
      "bbox": [
        307.08,
        569.77,
        542.11,
        651.71
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1228,
        2279,
        2168,
        2606
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 350,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2102.03334_p2_b37",
      "text": "CLIP (Radford et al., 2021) belongs to Figure 2b as it uses\nseparate but equally expensive transformer embedders for\neach modality. Interaction between the pooled image vec-\ntor and text vector is still shallow (dot product). Despite\nCLIP\u2019s remarkable zero-shot performance on image-to-text\n",
